{
 "figure": "fig1_strategy_evolution",
 "question": "How does the learned IPD strategy evolve over training, and does it evolve differently when the scripted opponent is grim trigger (never forgives) versus tit-for-tat (forgives on return)? Baseline arm only: grim/base vs tft/base.",
 "answer": {
  "summary": "No. Both arms converge on the same strategy -- cooperate almost throughout, then defect in the final round. Late-training (steps >= 25) cooperation is 0.908 +/- 0.017 for grim/base and 0.897 +/- 0.010 for tft/base, both arms open with C in 100% of episodes, and ever_defect equals defects_last_round to three decimals in every cell of the cache. That equality means every episode that defects at all ALSO defects in the final round -- no episode defects mid-game without defecting at the buzzer too -- and NOT that the final round is the only place defection ever lands. It is not: defect_before_last is 0.121 +/- 0.072 for grim/base and 0.104 +/- 0.032 for tft/base, so a further 12.1% and 10.4% of episodes (15.3% and 11.3% of the episodes that defect at all) carry one additional defection before the final round. The identity n_defects - defects_last_round = defect_before_last holds to 1e-09 in all 5 populated pooled_late cells. Early defection on this page is rare, not absent.",
  "where_they_separate": "defect_before_last is the only behaviour that responds to the opponent at all, and even it is small: grim/base 0.121 +/- 0.072 vs tft/base 0.104 +/- 0.032 late; the early-training curves separate more (tft runs higher through step 10) but the seed bands overlap throughout.",
  "degenerate_panel": "Panel 6 (rounds_in_punishment) is effectively empty: grim/base is identically 0.000 at every step and every seed, and tft/base is 0.0023 late. The reason is NOT that defection only ever happens in the final round -- it happens earlier in 12.1% of grim/base and 10.4% of tft/base episodes. It is that those early defections land so close to the buzzer that the opponent's retaliation is itself among the last moves of the game: conditional on defecting, the first defection sits 0.137 (grim/base) and 0.133 (tft/base) rounds from the end of a 10-round episode. With essentially no rounds left after the opponent answers, the arms never spend time in the regime where 'never forgives' and 'forgives' differ. That is the mechanism behind the null in panels 1-5, not a missing measurement.",
  "equality_reading": "ever_defect == defects_last_round is the statement that no episode defects mid-game without also defecting at the buzzer. It is strictly weaker than 'all defection is final-round defection', and the cache refutes that stronger reading in 3 of the 5 populated pooled_late cells (grim/base, tft/base, tft/eg), where defect_before_last is nonzero and is recovered exactly by n_defects - defects_last_round. See defect_before_last_identity_by_cell.",
  "ever_defect_equals_defects_last_round_by_cell": {
   "grim/base": true,
   "grim/eg": true,
   "grim/inf": true,
   "tft/base": true,
   "tft/eg": true,
   "tft/inf": true
  },
